  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mashood vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 18Th Day Of January 2016/28Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3840 of 2009 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 413/2008 Of The Additional Sessions Court(Adhoc)-Ii, Kozhikode Cc 210/2003 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-I, Koyilandy Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused: --------------------------------------- Mashood, S/O.Mammad Koya, Panai House, Thiruvangoor P.O., Koyilandy Taluk. By Advs.Sri.M.Asokan Sri.Devaprasanth.P.J. Respondent/Respondent/Complainant: -------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Koyilandy Circle, Kozhikode District. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala. By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 18-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. Mashood vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/82612478/ 1 B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P.No. 3840 of 2009 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 18th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The first accused in C.C.No.210 of 2003 on the files of the Court of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class, Koyilandi, has filed this revision petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Section 16 (1-A) (i) r/w Section 7(i) and 2(ia) (b) and (j) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act and Rule 29 and Rule 5 of Appendix B, para A.16.06.10 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules. 2. Heard. 3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 6.1.2003 at about 12.30 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 1 Kg of green gram dhal from the shop of the first accused. The first accused was the salesman cum licencee of the said shop. The second accused was the manufacturer of the food item. After taking the samples as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. Ext.P12 report of the public analyst would show that the sample contained synthetic food colour tartrazine under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory on the request of the accused. As per Ext.P22 certificate of analysis, the sample contained synthetic food colour tartrazine and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts. P1 to P23 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 to DW6 were examined and Exts.D1 to D10 were marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P22 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P22 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Mashood vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/82612478/ 2 Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P22 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Therefore, Ext. P22 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Mashood vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/82612478/ 2 Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
